---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const district = assertion('the-county-is-its-own-house-district')
const four = assertion('four-contests-with-one-name-on-the-ballot')
const roll = assertion('a-fifth-will-not-mark-an-unopposed-line')
const one = assertion('one-seat-one-man-four-years-apart')
const share = assertion('the-fourth-district-for-twenty-years')
const jordan = assertion('one-name-on-this-ballot-for-twenty-years')
const brown = assertion('sherrod-brown-came-closest')

const spec = (a: typeof roll, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const rollSpec = spec(roll, 'Per cent of the top-of-ticket vote not cast')
const oneSpec = spec(one, 'Per cent of voters skipping the race')
const shareSpec = spec(share, 'Republican share of the vote for Congress')
const brownSpec = spec(brown, 'Democratic share of the Senate vote')
---

<Article slug="one-name-on-the-ballot">
  <p>
    Ohio draws ninety-nine House districts across eighty-eight counties, so most of them cut a county
    in half or gather three together. Allen County is one of the exceptions, and the coincidence is
    exact: the district and the county are the same ground, which means one seat in Columbus is
    elected by this county and by nobody else.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={district} open />
  <p>
    That is a fair amount of power for a county of a hundred thousand people to hold on its own, and
    the returns say something uncomfortable about what it has done with it. Two men have held the
    House seat and the county's share of the 12th senate district between them for most of the
    century so far, and they have swapped chambers: Matt Huffman was the representative in 2006,
    2008 and 2012 and the senator in 2016 and 2020, while Bob Cupp has been the representative since
    2014. Ohio limits a member to eight consecutive years in a chamber, which is a reason a rotation
    of this shape happens and not evidence that it is why this one did.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={four} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="roll"
    title="What happens when nobody runs"
    caption="Votes cast for the Ohio House seat as a shortfall against the highest-voted statewide race on the same ballot. The three tall bars are the years the ballot line carried one name."
    columns={['Year', 'Shortfall']}
    rows={roll.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="roll" set:html={JSON.stringify(rollSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={roll} />
  <p>
    In 2016 Bob Cupp received every vote cast for the Ohio House in this county, which is a hundred
    per cent of them and 34,564 of the 45,894 people who came to the polls. Eleven thousand three
    hundred and thirty voters marked a ballot and left that line alone. The two largest votes any
    candidate in these returns received — Huffman's 39,979 in 2008 and Cupp's 38,882 in 2020 — were
    both cast against nobody, which is worth holding onto the next time a big number is read as a
    mandate.
  </p>
  <Gloss>
    So the corpus will not print the share. A vote share measures a preference only where a
    preference could be expressed, and a series that admits an uncontested year reports it as the
    most one-sided election in the run — when what it actually was is the election that stopped
    being an election. Where the line carried one name, the node publishes the count, publishes the
    roll-off, and says the line was uncontested. Establishing that takes the statewide file, not the
    county's rows: a race with one candidate everywhere is uncontested, and a race with one candidate
    in one county is a transcription that lost a line.
  </Gloss>
  <AssertionCard assertion={one} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="one"
    title="The same seat, four years apart"
    caption="Allen County voters skipping the 12th senate district race. The candidate is Matt Huffman in both years; the difference is whether anyone stood against him."
    columns={['Year', 'Skipped']}
    rows={one.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="one" set:html={JSON.stringify(oneSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    One office, one man, two elections. The only thing that changed between them is whether the
    ballot offered an alternative, and a fifth of the county's voters changed their behaviour with
    it. Whatever that fifth were expressing, it was not enthusiasm.
  </p>

  <h2>The federal races, which are always contested</h2>
  <AssertionCard assertion={share} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="share"
    title="Twenty years of voting for Congress"
    caption="Republican share of every vote cast in the race, ten general elections. Allen County was in Ohio's 4th district throughout."
    columns={['Year', 'Share']}
    rows={share.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="share" set:html={JSON.stringify(shareSpec)} is:inline />
  <AssertionCard assertion={jordan} />
  <p>
    The congressional race, unlike the legislative one, holds nearly everyone who turns up: its total
    is within four per cent of the top of the ticket in every one of the ten years. A contest people
    can lose is a contest people vote in.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={brown} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="brown"
    title="The nearest a Democrat has come"
    caption="Sherrod Brown's share of Allen County's Senate vote. 2006 is also the year the county gave its governor's vote to a Democrat, by 816."
    columns={['Year', 'Share']}
    rows={brown.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="brown" set:html={JSON.stringify(brownSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    Which places 2006 as this county's one modern opening, and it closed. In that November Allen gave
    a Democrat the governorship, held its Senate Republican by eight points rather than the usual
    twenty, and elected the congressman it has returned seven times since by the smallest margin he
    has ever taken here.
  </p>
</Article>
